{
  "title": "Project: Fundamental Circuit Analysis via KVL and KCL",
  "body": "This project requires students to apply the Basic KVL/KCL method of circuit analysis to solve for unknown voltages and currents in linear circuits. Students must first select a reference node (ground) and label node voltages as primary unknowns. They will then write Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) equations for all non-ground nodes, substituting device laws (Ohm's Law for resistors, constraints for sources) and Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL) where necessary. The final step involves solving the resulting system of algebraic equations to find node voltages, followed by back-solving for branch currents and voltages. Students must strictly adhere to the 'associated variables discipline,' ensuring current is defined as positive entering the positive voltage terminal to correctly calculate power consumption. The project may also involve simplifying circuits using element combination rules (series/parallel resistors, current/voltage sources) before applying the full KVL/KCL method, though the primary focus is on the systematic node-based approach described in the lecture notes.",
