{
  "title": "Draft Instructor Guide: Lecture 1 - Receptors and Dose-Response Concepts",
  "body": "This lecture introduces fundamental pharmacological definitions essential for the remainder of the course. We will define agonists as drugs that bind to receptors to produce a characteristic effect, distinguishing between full and partial agonists based on maximal effect. Antagonists bind without causing an effect, thereby blocking active substances; these may be competitive, non-competitive, or irreversible. The core concept of dose-response is introduced, noting that response typically increases with dose, though the curve is often sigmoidal when effect is plotted against log dose. We will measure effects as either graded variables (e.g., blood pressure changes) or quantal variables (e.g., mortality). Key metrics include the ED50 (median effective dose), LD50 (lethal dose), and EC50 (effective concentration). We will clarify the distinction between potency (dose required for a specific effect intensity) and efficacy (maximum effect capability), noting that a potent drug may have low efficacy. Affinity refers to binding strength quantified by the dissociation constant (kD), while selectivity describes the separation between desired and undesired effects. Finally, we will briefly review the pharmacokinetic journey of a drug, from absorption and distribution into peripheral tissues (acting as reservoirs) to biotransformation, excretion, and the potential for drug-receptor interaction at the target site.",
  "citations": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
}

{
  "title": "Course FAQs: Pharmacology Fundamentals",
  "body": "Q: What is the difference between an agonist and an antagonist? A: An agonist is a drug that binds to a receptor and produces a characteristic effect, which can be a full or partial effect depending on the maximal response. An antagonist binds to the receptor without causing an effect, thereby preventing an active substance from gaining access. Antagonists may be competitive, non-competitive, or irreversible. [1] Q: How is drug potency defined and measured? A: Potency refers to the dose (or molar concentration) required to produce a specific intensity of effect. It is commonly specified using the ED50 (median effective dose), which is the dose producing a response in 50% of subjects. Relative potency applies specifically when comparing drugs acting by the same mechanism, indicated by parallel dose-response curves. [2][5] Q: What is the difference between efficacy and potency? A: Efficacy (or maximal efficacy/intrinsic activity) is the maximum effect a drug is capable of producing, whereas potency is the amount of drug needed to achieve a specific effect. A potent drug may have low efficacy, and a highly efficacious drug may have low potency. Clinically, efficacy is often more important than potency. [3] Q: What does selectivity mean in pharmacology? A: Selectivity refers to the separation between desired and undesired effects of a drug. Ideally, a drug is completely specific, but many therapeutic agents produce dose-related side effects near their therapeutic range. [4] Q: What factors influence the distribution of a drug in the body? A: After administration, unbound drugs follow their concentration gradient to distribute into peripheral tissues. Some tissues contain the target site, while others act as non-specific binding reservoirs. The total volume of distribution determines the equilibrium concentration of the drug after a specified dose. [7] Q: How are drug effects typically measured? A: Effects may be measured as a graded variable (e.g., change in blood pressure, force of contraction) or as a quantal variable (e.g., number dead/alive). When plotted against the log dose, the resulting curve is usually sigmoidal. [2] Q: What is the role of the receptor in drug action? A: In most cases, the site of action is a macromolecular receptor located in the target tissue. Drug effects are usually temporary because the body has systems for detoxification and elimination. [8]",
  "citations": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]
}

