{
  "title": "Pharmacokinetics I & II: Project Directions and Learning Outcomes",
  "body": "This project requires students to analyze the relationship between pharmacokinetic parameters and plasma drug concentrations. Students must first define total clearance as the volume of plasma completely cleared of drug per unit time. They will then explain how dose, bioavailability, rate of absorption, apparent volume of distribution, total clearance, and elimination half-life affect plasma concentrations following a single dose. The project involves calculating the elimination rate constant and half-life using first-order elimination kinetics, where the elimination rate is proportional to plasma concentration. Students must determine renal clearance by measuring the amount of drug excreted in urine over a time interval and interpolating plasma concentration at the midpoint. Furthermore, they will calculate the apparent volume of distribution by extrapolating the line of best fit for ln Cp vs t data to time zero. Finally, students will analyze steady-state conditions, demonstrating that plasma concentration at steady-state is directly proportional to the input rate and inversely proportional to total plasma clearance, while noting that half-life is directly proportional to volume of distribution and inversely proportional to total clearance.",
  "citations": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
}

{
  "title": "Instructor Draft: Pharmacokinetic Principles and Calculations",
  "body": "This module covers the fundamental parameters of pharmacokinetics, specifically focusing on the relationships between dose, bioavailability, and plasma concentrations. Students must understand how the apparent volume of distribution (Vd), total clearance (ClT), and elimination half-life (t1/2) interact to determine drug behavior after administration. First-order elimination kinetics are assumed, where the elimination rate is proportional to plasma concentration, resulting in a constant half-life independent of dose. The course will detail the mathematical determination of renal clearance, apparent volume of distribution via extrapolation to time zero, and total clearance. Furthermore, the impact of infusion rates and multiple dosing on steady-state plasma concentrations (Css) will be analyzed, emphasizing that Css is directly proportional to the input rate and inversely proportional to total clearance. Key relationships, such as the dependence of peak plasma concentration on absorption and elimination half-lives, and the calculation of Area Under the Curve (AUC) based on dose and bioavailability, will be reviewed.",
  "citations": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
}

